+The unrestricted advantage is concentrated in the first term. Mean reductions are 0.00535, 0.01163 and 0.00363 for SPY, QQQ and IWM, and the SPY and QQQ intervals lie below zero. The conditional term moves by $+0.00004$, $-0.00007$ and $-0.00131$; only the IWM interval excludes zero, and it does so against the model. The position rule depends on $p_{t,3}-p_{t,1}=(1-m_t)(2a_t-1)$, so an improvement confined to $m_t$ reaches the allocation only through a multiplier near one half. Conditionally on these forecasts, the part of the score that improves is not the part that the allocation weighs most directly.
+
+The reversible chain reproduces the first term, with differences from the unrestricted chain of $-0.00013$, $+0.00034$ and $-0.00008$ whose intervals all contain zero, and loses ground in the second, by $+0.00144$, $+0.00035$ and $+0.00167$ with the SPY and IWM intervals above zero. That second term is where the unrestricted chain gained nothing, and it is also where the directional asymmetry removed by detailed balance is encoded. The ordering of total log loss is therefore reproduced at the component level: the restriction costs a little exactly where the unrestricted chain was not gaining. These are conditional descriptions of saved forecasts, not tests of a mechanism.
