+\subsection{What detailed balance removes: circulation flows}\label{sec:circulation}
+For a stationary distribution $\pi$ and transition matrix $P$, the net probability flow from $i$ to $j$ is $J_{ij}=\pi_iP_{ij}-\pi_jP_{ji}$. Stationarity makes the three independent entries equal, $J_{12}=J_{23}=J_{31}=a_t$, so one scalar measures the mass rotating around the state cycle at each origin. Detailed balance is $a_t=0$, which the reversible parameterization imposes by construction. Because terciles are equal-count cutpoints, the fitted stationary distribution stays close to uniform and $a_t$ is approximately one third of the corresponding pairwise probability difference.
+
+Figure~\ref{fig:circulation} plots $a_t$ for the three assets and Table~\ref{tab:circulation} summarizes it. The scalar is negative at every one of the 2,766 evaluation origins for every asset, with means of $-1.40$, $-0.77$ and $-1.32$ percentage points of daily flow. In the period-average matrices the three pairwise differences have nearly equal magnitude: for SPY, $P(\text{Up}\mid\text{Down})-P(\text{Down}\mid\text{Up})=+4.2$ and the two other pairs are $-4.2$ percentage points; the corresponding magnitudes are 2.3 for QQQ and 4.0 for IWM. The reversible fit replaces each asymmetric pair by its average, shifting individual off-diagonal entries by 1.2 to 2.1 percentage points.
+
+The series is highly persistent, with first-order autocorrelation between 0.990 and 0.997. The agreement in sign across origins is therefore not 2,766 independent confirmations; it describes one persistent feature of this sample, and the annual means move by at most a few tenths of a point. Removing that feature costs 0.0007 to 0.0016 nats of log loss, an interval that contains zero (Table~\ref{tab:intervals}). The asymmetry is present in these data and the restriction suppresses it; whether suppressing it explains the ranking would require the controlled simulation that this study does not provide. No causal reading of the circulation is offered.
+
